The task of open-domain commonsense question answering (CQA) involves retrieving relevant information from a vast knowledge base to answer questions that require common sense. However, the complexity of this task stems from the vastness of the knowledge base and the need to identify relevant information. To address this challenge, we propose a novel approach that leverages a coarse-to-precise framework to narrow down the search space. This framework involves two stages: the first stage uses a coarse-grained semantic search to identify a set of relevant passages, and the second stage employs a fine-grained semantic search to pinpoint the most relevant passage. Our approach is evaluated on the CQA dataset and outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods. Furthermore, we conduct an ablation study to demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach and provide insights into the importance of semantic search in CQA. Our code is available at https://github.com/chenxinyu1995/Coarse-to-Precise. 

